1
00:03:08,571 --> 00:03:14,571
An eclipse awakens an ancient monster whose gaze turns flesh into stone.

2
00:06:17,142 --> 00:06:23,142
Basilisk: The Serpent King is a action, horror, tv
movie film directed by Stephen Furst.

3
00:09:25,714 --> 00:09:31,714
Two millennia ago, a Lybian king has a basilisk (snake-shaped dragon), which petrifies
people, subjected to the same fate with a golden scepter during a solar eclipse.

4
00:12:34,285 --> 00:12:40,285
Both these and several victims are dug up by modern
archaeologist Harrison 'Harry' McColl's expedition.

5
00:15:42,857 --> 00:15:48,857
Despite a cryptic warning from tribal locals, everything
goes to his Colorado university's museum.

6
00:18:51,428 --> 00:18:57,428
It's all exhibited during another eclipse, which leads to the monster reviving.

7
00:22:00,000 --> 00:22:06,000
Harry and some of his friends must try to petrify the monster again.

8
00:25:08,571 --> 00:25:14,571
Dr. Harrison 'Harry' McColl, played by Jeremy London.

9
00:28:17,142 --> 00:28:23,142
Rachel Donegal, played by Wendy Carter.

10
00:31:25,714 --> 00:31:31,714
Col. Douglas, played by Cleavant Derricks.

11
00:34:34,285 --> 00:34:40,285
Rudy Skeeters, played by Griff Furst.

12
00:37:42,857 --> 00:37:48,857
Sierra, played by Sarah Skeeters.

13
00:40:51,428 --> 00:40:57,428
Carlton, played by Stephen Furst.

14
00:44:00,000 --> 00:44:06,000
Serpents (Hebrew: נָחָשׁ, romanized: nāḥāš) are referred to in
both the Hebrew Bible and the New Testament.

15
00:47:08,571 --> 00:47:14,571
The symbol of a serpent or snake played important roles in the religious traditions
and cultural life of ancient Greece, Egypt, Mesopotamia, and Canaan.

16
00:50:17,142 --> 00:50:23,142
The serpent was a symbol of evil power and chaos from the underworld as
well as a symbol of fertility, life, healing, and rebirth.

17
00:53:25,714 --> 00:53:31,714
Nāḥāš (נחש‎), Hebrew for "snake", is also associated with divination, including
the verb form meaning "to practice divination or fortune-telling".

18
00:56:34,285 --> 00:56:40,285
Nāḥāš occurs in the Torah to identify the serpent in the Garden of Eden.

19
00:59:42,857 --> 00:59:48,857
Throughout the Hebrew Bible, it is also used in conjunction with
seraph to describe vicious serpents in the wilderness.

20
01:02:51,428 --> 01:02:57,428
The tannin, a dragon monster, also occurs throughout the Hebrew Bible.

21
01:06:00,000 --> 01:06:06,000
In the Book of Exodus, the staves of Moses and Aaron are turned
into serpents, a nāḥāš for Moses, a tannin for Aaron.

22
01:09:08,571 --> 01:09:14,571
In the New Testament, the Book of Revelation makes use of ancient serpent and the Dragon
several times to identify Satan or the Devil (Revelation 12:9; 20:2).

23
01:12:17,142 --> 01:12:23,142
The serpent is most often identified with the
hubristic Satan, and sometimes with Lilith.

24
01:15:25,714 --> 01:15:31,714
The narrative of the Garden of Eden and the fall of humankind constitute a mythological tradition shared by all the Abrahamic religions, with a presentation more or less symbolic of
Abrahamic morals and religious beliefs, which had an overwhelming impact on human sexuality, gender roles, and sex differences both in the Western and Islamic civilizations.

25
01:18:34,285 --> 01:18:40,285
In mainstream (Nicene) Christianity, the doctrine of the Fall is
closely related to that of original sin or ancestral sin.

26
01:21:42,857 --> 01:21:48,857
Unlike Christianity, the other major Abrahamic religions, Judaism and Islam, do not have a concept of
"original sin", and instead have developed varying other interpretations of the Eden narrative.

27
01:24:51,428 --> 01:24:57,428
The tone is dark.
